        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": "The breakdown ruptured not only a local infrastructure, but two dominant narratives that had underpinned China’s digital governance for an extended period of time. First, it overturned the long arc of “big data” triumphalism that had celebrated databases as seamless, depoliticized engines of growth and optimization. Since the early 2010s, state media and consultancy reports alike portrayed China’s data architecture as a model of frictionless coordination—linking platforms, citizens, and regulators with unprecedented efficiency. {{note:7}} Second, and more acutely, the Xi’an failure punctured the pandemic-era fantasy of China exceptionalism: the widely touted idea that China, unlike the United States and the United Kingdom, could harness real-time digital infrastructure to manage crisis at scale. The health-code system had come to symbolize this distinction, functioning as both a biopolitical credential and a nationalist credential—proof that China’s infrastructural capacity could outperform the pluralist chaos of Western public health. {{note:8}} But Xi’an reversed that script. The very infrastructure once used to justify state competence now made visible its brittleness. Scholars have since traced this shift—from “data as accelerator” to “data as access infrastructure”—arguing that the same logic which once enabled mobility now served as a checkpoint of exclusion. {{note:9}}"
