        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": "The conventional account typically minimizes alternative trajectories. Soviet cybernetic planners sketched OGAS (1962–1970) to network factory computers across the USSR but were stymied by bureaucratic rivalries and foreign-exchange shortages. Crucially, this historiographical asymmetry did not apply only to socialist states. Even among close U.S. allies such as France, West Germany, and Japan, efforts to chart alternative technological futures were often depicted as provincial, derivative, or ultimately assimilated into a presumed Anglo-American trajectory. France’s Plan Calcul (1966–1975) and the German language-processing project LILOG (1985–1991) sought to displace Anglo-American standards yet ultimately fed knowledge back to IBM and Oracle via collaboration agreements. Japan’s Trusted Information Infrastructure initiative (mid-1990s) likewise framed database research as national security, anticipating twenty-first-century talk of “data localization.” These important experiments—many of which failed—were seldom afforded the same retrospective seriousness as “failed” U.S. initiatives, which are often cast as generative dead ends or necessary precursors. In this mode of narration, failure in the periphery is more often read as confirmation of incapacity, rather than as evidence of epistemic divergence or structural constraint."
