        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": "By the late 1970s, when China’s leadership began reevaluating economic governance, these intellectual fragments provided an important foundation for renewed efforts to integrate computational data processing into economic management. Figures like Wu Jiapei, Sun Yefang, and Yu Guangyuan, who had been involved in early explorations of mathematical modeling before the Sino-Soviet Split, emerged as key interlocutors and architects in adapting past approaches to the new political and technological landscape. {{note:141}} Once-discredited scholars like Lawrence Klein would later become some of the CAS’s most esteemed guests. {{note:142}} The Huangshan Conference, rather than marking a radical new beginning, reflected this intellectual reactivation—a process in which pre-Cultural Revolution ideas were selectively revived, reinterpreted, and aligned with the emerging discourse on economic modernization and database science."
