        "text": "Concerns over paper quality also reinforced the NBS officials’ cautious stance toward alternatives like FOSDIC, following their rejection of punch cards. During the 1970s, China had been actively exploring advancements in optical sensing and optical recognition (OCR) technologies, with Fudan University’s 789 system serving as a notable example. This model could reportedly process 100 characters per second, achieving an accuracy rate comparable to IBM’s 1975 Optical Page Reader (in controlled environments). However, its performance was also highly dependent on the consistency of ink marks and the physical condition of the paper. Wrinkles and folds—inevitable in the handling of 220 million sheets of paper during data collection—further undermined its reliability. Moreover, the high cost of such technologies, exemplified by the IBM Optical Page Reader System’s price tag of over three million dollars—which was ten times the price of the entry-level IBM 4300 series—placed them out of reach for widespread adoption in China. While computers purchased for census purposes could be repurposed for a variety of other high-value applications, optical recognition machines lacked similar versatility, limiting their utility and further discouraging investment in these systems. For China’s research on OCR and FOS-DIC-like systems, see for instance, Xin Zhou 周新 and Leqiu Qian 钱乐秋, “Guangxue wenzi shibie xitong 光学⽂字识别系统 [Optical Character Recognition System],” Zidonghua xuebao ⾃ 动化学报 [ACTA Automatica Sinica] 5, no.1 (1979): 30-8. Also see Zonghui Zhang 张宗惠, “789 feidian saomiaoqi 789 飞点扫描器 [The Flying Spot Scanner of the 789 model],” Zidonghua xuebao 5, no.2 (1979): 1-24. For NBS’s concerns of the technology, see Zhou, “Dianzi jisuanji zai renkou pucha shuju chuli fangmian de yingyong,” 25."
